# PlantOps admin console — environment template (Doc 09)
#
# Copy to `apps/admin-web/.env.local` and adjust. Next.js loads `.env*` from
# this directory; the workspace-root `.env` is the API's, validated by
# `libs/config`, and deliberately holds nothing the browser reads.
#
# Everything here is `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, which means Next inlines it into the
# client bundle at build time. That is not a leak, it is the definition: these
# values are as public as the network tab. Nothing secret may be added to this
# file — a secret on a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable is a secret published to every
# visitor.

# Where the browser reaches the IAM. No `/iam` or `/auth` suffix.
#
# OPTIONAL. Left unset, the console calls the same-origin path `/api`, which is
# what makes one build serve any hostname (Doc 11 §3, §8): the value baked into
# the bundle is true everywhere, and the proxy in front of the deployment maps
# `/api` to the API — stripping the prefix, since the API serves `/iam`,
# `/auth`, `/health` and `/ready` at its root. A same-origin console needs no
# `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` entry at all.
#
# Set it only when the console and the API are on *different* origins. Local
# development is exactly that case, and is why this template ships a value: the
# console's dev server holds 4200 so it does not collide with the API's PORT,
# nothing proxies between them, and the API's CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS therefore
# names http://localhost:4200. A managed deployment that hosts the console
# separately from the API (Vercel in front of Railway) is the same case: set the
# API's URL here, and add this console's origin to CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS there,
# or the browser blocks every request before it is sent.
NEXT_PUBLIC_IAM_API_URL=http://localhost:3000
